WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.664
Batteries are highly regulated.

00:00:03.664 --> 00:00:13.740
To ensure they are properly categorized, we need to collect specific

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.353
details about the battery.

00:00:15.353 --> 00:00:21.000
When selling a battery or a product with a battery, you will be asked

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.388
a few additional questions.

00:00:23.388 --> 00:00:27.566
You are required to provide accurate information.

00:00:27.566 --> 00:00:28.760
Missing or

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:35.300
inaccurate information can lead to safety incidents, delays in setting up the product

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.786
and getting it to customers quickly, and regulatory action.

00:00:39.786 --> 00:00:41.282
Let's get started.

00:00:41.282 --> 00:00:41.780
What

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.367
is the composition of the battery you are selling?

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.360
Common composition types are Lithium

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.240
ion, Lithium metal, alkaline, and nickel metal hydride.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.900
Most of the time, the composition

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.810
will be printed on the battery.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:03.660
Or, it can also be found in the manufacturer's documentation.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:08.778
If you are selling a Lithium battery, we will ask you a few more questions.

00:01:08.778 --> 00:01:09.460
How many

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.604
batteries are included in your listing?

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:14.820
The number of batteries is a critical piece of

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.076
information for us.

00:01:16.076 --> 00:01:21.940
Let's take a look at a listing for a set of walkie-talkie radios.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.800
At first glance, you may think of each radio using one battery.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.420
Really, the complete listing

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:34.020
contains two batteries, so your listing should specify two batteries.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.820
You will also be asked

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:39.353
about the number of cells.

00:01:39.353 --> 00:01:44.420
Battery regulations make a distinction between a single cell battery

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.607
and a multi-cell battery.

00:01:46.607 --> 00:01:50.980
Common examples of single cell batteries would be the double

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:56.745
A batteries used in a flashlight, or the coin cell found in your watch.

00:01:56.745 --> 00:01:57.980
A common example

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:02.600
of a multi-cell battery is the battery in your laptop.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.700
Laptop batteries can have up

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:12.180
to nine Lithium ion cells linked together within the plastic battery casing to provide

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.564
longer battery life.

00:02:13.564 --> 00:02:19.100
The number of cells for multi-cell batteries can typically be found

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.420
on the manufacturer's website or in their product documentation.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.340
What is the weight

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.513
of the battery or batteries?

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:31.860
This refers to the total weight of all Lithium batteries

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.506
minus the product.

00:02:33.506 --> 00:02:39.540
Batteries can be weighed manually, but this should also be available

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.740
in the manufacturer's documentation.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:44.490
What is the battery type?

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.140
Common battery types

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:54.950
are double A, triple A, C-R-1-2-3-A, C-R-2, and 9 volt.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:58.060
The battery type is usually printed

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:03.532
on the battery, or it can be found in the manufacturer's documentation.

00:03:03.532 --> 00:03:04.900
We will also

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.297
ask you how the battery is packaged.

00:03:08.297 --> 00:03:12.180
There are three ways batteries are typically shipped.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:18.814
Batteries only, batteries contained in equipment, and batteries packed with equipment.

00:03:18.814 --> 00:03:20.020
If you

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:24.574
are selling stand-alone batteries, select batteries only.

00:03:24.574 --> 00:03:27.420
For example, a spare laptop

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:34.460
battery, or an external battery charger like a power bank, or if you are including other

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:42.100
products not used by the battery like a bonus charging cord, even a power bank integrated

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:48.980
into a product like a purse for convenient charging is considered batteries only when

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:50.411
answering this question.

00:03:50.411 --> 00:03:55.180
Select batteries contained in equipment if you are selling a

00:03:55.180 --> 00:04:01.281
battery that is pre-installed in the product or not removable.

00:04:01.281 --> 00:04:03.500
For example, tablets, solar

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:08.567
lights, wireless speakers, LED candles, and wireless headphones.

00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:11.100
Though you can't see

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:18.540
the battery, these products do contain batteries and are categorized as batteries contained

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:19.449
in equipment.

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:24.900
Select batteries packed with equipment if your listing includes a battery

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.620
shipped with the product and it is not pre-installed.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.980
Everyday items that commonly come

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:39.891
with a battery packed alongside it are power tools, flashlights, and digital cameras.

00:04:39.891 --> 00:04:40.500
Next,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:45.112
you will be asked to provide the watt hour rating.

00:04:45.112 --> 00:04:47.880
Battery sales are regulated and restricted

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.798
based on the watt hour rating.

00:04:50.798 --> 00:04:55.660
The maximum allowable watt hour rating can vary by country,

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:00.500
so check Seller Central to ensure that your battery complies with the battery selling

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:02.330
policy for your marketplace.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:07.820
You can find the watt hours printed on the battery or on

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.005
the battery pack.

00:05:09.005 --> 00:05:14.140
If the watt hours are not printed on the battery and the manufacturer's

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:22.051
documentation is not available, you can calculate watt hours yourself using this formula.

00:05:22.051 --> 00:05:27.737
Milliamp hours divided by 1000 times the voltage.

00:05:27.737 --> 00:05:32.180
Check Seller Central Help for more information.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:39.060
Lithium metal battery sales are also regulated and restricted based on the grams of lithium

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.340
contained in the battery.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:45.330
For this information, check the manufacturer's documentation.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:45.900
If

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:51.900
the lithium content is not available in the documentation, then it can be calculated using

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.204
this formula.

00:05:53.204 --> 00:06:02.980
Milliamp hours divided by 1000 times 0.3 to give you the lithium content in

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:03.410
grams.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.990
Need more help answering these questions?

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:09.860
Go to help in Seller Central and videos on

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:10.751
Seller University.

00:06:10.751 --> 00:06:13.871
Both are great resources for learning more.

00:06:13.871 --> 00:06:16.100
Thank you for helping to

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:22.620
keep our communities and our customers safe.
